#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124263 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/id #uuid "c262f28d-bfd0-5c78-bce7-d2ae314052e1" :question/math-academy-id "e-5510" :question/problem "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $A^{-1}=pI+qA$, find the value of $p+q$." :question/worked-solution "Let's find the characteristic equation\n\n$$\n\\textrm{det}(A-\\lambda I)=0\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ndet(A - λI) &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}-8 - λ & -9 \\\\ 6 & 7 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(-8 - λ)(7 - λ) + 54 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} + λ - 2 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{2} + A - 2I &= O\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $A$ is invertible, we can multiply this equation by $A^{-1}$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA^{-1}(A^{2} + A - 2I) &= O \\\\\nA + I - 2A^{-1} &= O \\\\\nA^{-1} &= \\frac{1}{2}A + \\frac{1}{2}I\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\n$$\nq=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\np+q=\\dfrac{1}{2}+\\dfrac{1}{2}=1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124264 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b2867571-c1ae-5ea0-b784-22f444c8db38" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Power of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Smaller Powers"}] :question/id #uuid "8e6d3017-d05d-5a2d-8043-c827e612476e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5511" :question/problem "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix such that $\\textrm{tr}(A)=-4$ and $\\textrm{det}(A)=-18$. If $A^2 = xA + yI$ for constants $x$ and $y$, then find the value of $x+y$." :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\np(\\lambda)=\\lambda^2-\\textrm{tr}(A)\\lambda+\\textrm{det}(A)\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(A) &= O \\\\\nA^{2} - tr(A)A + det(A)I &= O \\\\\nA^{2} &= tr(A)A - det(A)I \\\\\nA^{2} &= -4A + 18I\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nx+y = -4+18 = 14\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124265 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "787173e1-f957-5ff0-9d14-a6ccb11de1d8" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Matrix Polynomial Using the Cayley-Hamilton Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "ce724b3b-f440-5c1f-86a6-c50a54931233" :question/math-academy-id "e-5512" :question/problem "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$ and $f(x) = x^{12} -x^{10} +2x-1$, find $f(A)$." :question/worked-solution "Let's start by finding the characteristic polynomial $p(\\lambda)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(λ) &= det(A - λI) \\\\\n&=\\begin{vmatrix}4 - λ & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4 - λ\\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= (4 - λ)(-4 - λ) + 15 \\\\\n&= λ^{2} - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's divide $f(x)$ by $p(x)$ using long division:\n\n|  | $\\:\\:x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $x^2-1\\:$ | $\\!\\require{enclose}\\enclose{longdiv}{x^{12} \\,-\\, x^{10} + 2x - 1 {\\phantom{\\mid}}}$ |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n|  | $x^{12}$ | $-$ | $x^{10}$ |  |  |  |  |\n|  |  |  |  |  | $2x$ | $-$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, we can write $f(x)$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= q(x)p(x) + r(x) \\\\\n&= x^{10}(x^{2} - 1) + (2x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nq(x) = x^{10}\n$$\n\nReplacing $x$ with $A$, we have\n\n$$\nf(A) = q(A)p(A)+(2A-I)\n$$\n\nAccording to the Cayley-Hamilton theorem, we have $p(A)=O$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(A) &= q(A)p(A) + (2A - I) \\\\\n&= q(A) \\cdot O + (2A - I) \\\\\n&= 2A - I \\\\\n&= 2\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}8 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -8\\end{bmatrix} - \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}7 & -10 \\\\ 6 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124279 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "87febdb4-5f54-5aff-b84a-1b05359d6b57" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Sum and Product of Eigenvalues of a 2x2 Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "95b44b17-b796-51eb-93dd-4ee4b2e5c3f2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5894" :question/problem "Given that $\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$ find the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$." :question/worked-solution "The characteristic polynomial of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\np(λ) &= λ^{2} - tr(A)λ + det(A)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using Vieta's formulas, we have that the roots of $p(\\lambda)$, i.e., the eigenvalues of $A$, satisfy\n\n$$\n{λ_{1} + λ_{2} = tr(A); λ_{1} \\cdot λ_{2} = det(A)\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the eigenvalues of $A$ is equal to the trace of $A$.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2 = \\textrm{tr}(A)= 9+(-6)=3\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "152243fa-e0a5-551d-92d9-b470789087c7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Inverse of a Matrix as a Linear Combination of Its Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db022e60-b4a3-5fed-a684-66f4384b0da1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123101} {:answer/id #uuid "0fd446df-b6d7-5613-be91-a05c4299f3a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123102} {:answer/id #uuid "4a97d7f1-0d82-5c27-a742-610ee8832586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123103} {:answer/id #uuid "68aa5ebf-5e46-560a-9bcb-e8c435dcc742" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123104} {:answer/id #uuid "70868969-3b44-542c-836c-06a1bc12bd70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70868969-3b44-542c-836c-06a1bc12bd70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123105} :answer-field/id #uuid "2fc50ce1-88c5-57fe-b2fa-149c8b0f42fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123100}] :question/id #uuid "4acccd18-c797-5d41-ab58-67f98662f9b8" :question/math-academy-id "q-37779" :question/problem "Given that $A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{bmatrix}$ and $A^{-1} = pI + qA$, find the value of $p + q$."}]}
